    "content": "The proposed AI Tutor for MBA Students is assumed to operate primarily on a direct-to-consumer (D2C) subscription-based model, targeting individual MBA students in the United States. This model would likely feature tiered pricing, varying based on the specific features offered or the level of usage. This approach is a common strategy within the EdTech sector for generating recurring revenue, though the specific pricing, conversion rates, and customer acquisition volumes for MBA students remain unconfirmed assumptions.\n\nBeyond individual subscriptions, a secondary revenue stream could be explored through B2B partnerships with MBA programs or universities. In this scenario, the AI tutor might be licensed or integrated as an endorsed supplementary resource. Such institutional partnerships could provide legitimacy and broader distribution, and potentially address some regulatory concerns. However, the feasibility, specific pricing models for institutions, and potential revenue share from such collaborations are currently unconfirmed.\n\nThe cost structure is anticipated to involve significant investment in AI development, including large language model integration and specialized pedagogical algorithms, alongside ongoing maintenance and data security. Compliance with evolving educational AI regulations in the US will also represent a key cost component. The expected gross margin profile is currently speculative, as it depends heavily on unconfirmed pricing strategies and variable costs associated with serving each user.",
