  "go_to_market_strategy": {
    "title": "Go-to-Market Strategy",
    "content": "The initial go-to-market strategy will focus on directly engaging MBA students through digital channels, academic forums, and student networks within the United States. This approach aims to highlight the benefits of personalized learning support and efficiency that the AI tutor could offer, leveraging students' general familiarity with AI tools like ChatGPT [web-236a31a2f5a04bcb8b4ae6d39dee9e37]. Direct engagement is intended to bypass potential institutional adoption hurdles, though specific MBA student engagement channels and preferences are currently unconfirmed.\n\nAddressing the relatively low perception of AI benefits in education among the US public (only 39% consider it beneficial) [web-236a31a2f5a04bcb8b4ae6d39dee9e37] and the adoption gap among higher education faculty (only 22% regularly use generative AI compared to 83% of K-12 teachers) [web-236a31a2f5a04bcb8b4ae6d39dee9e37] will be critical. This requires developing clear communication strategies and product demonstrations that emphasize academic integrity, human oversight, and the tutor's role as a supplementary tool, not a replacement for faculty. The effectiveness of this trust-building strategy is hypothetical.\n\nFurthermore, the strategy must actively monitor and engage with the evolving federal and state AI in education regulations. All 50 US states have introduced legislation on AI, and federal executive orders have established a national framework for AI in education [web-57da4247bdb54dd99b32029fe644c91b]. Educational leaders in higher education are actively crafting policies focusing on innovation, academic integrity, data privacy, and human oversight [web-00ec92ece8e14231883ddb1c6047a8dd]. Seeking opportunities for early compliance and potential university certifications or endorsements will be crucial for long-term viability and institutional partnerships, though specific regulatory pathways and institutional receptiveness are unconfirmed.",
