    "content": "The financial projections for the AI Tutor for MBA Students are currently based on qualitative assumptions, as specific financial data, a defined business model, and market research on MBA student willingness to pay are not available. The primary revenue model is assumed to be a direct-to-consumer (D2C) subscription service for individual MBA students, with potential secondary revenue from B2B institutional partnerships. However, specific pricing tiers, conversion rates, and customer acquisition volumes are unconfirmed and require validation.\n\nSignificant costs are anticipated for AI development, including initial build-out, ongoing enhancements, and maintenance of core technology, as well as operational expenses like customer support, platform hosting, and data storage. Marketing and customer acquisition costs (CAC) will also be incurred to reach and convert MBA students. Furthermore, ongoing regulatory and compliance costs are expected due to the rapidly evolving AI in education landscape in the United States.\n\nUnit economics, including pricing strategy, gross margin, Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC), and Customer Lifetime Value (LTV), are currently speculative. The price per unit will need to be competitive and reflect perceived value, while gross margin will depend on subscription price and variable costs. LTV will be crucial, driven by retention rates and average revenue per user (ARPU), especially given the likely high CAC. Achieving break-even will depend on a critical balance of subscriber volume, subscription price, variable costs, and fixed costs.",
